# Default: treat all files as text, normalize EOLs to LF on checkin and checkout
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text files (belt and braces)
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# A GUARD, not a description of today. `docx-comments.ts` no longer holds raw NUL
# bytes — its importAnnotationId delimiter is written as the `\0` escape — so
# `git diff` renders it textually with or without this line. It stays because the
# raw form is a one-character edit away, and reintroducing it is silent: `text=auto`
# above marks a NUL-bearing file binary on content sniffing, and the file then
# shows only "Bin N -> M bytes" and goes INVISIBLE in every pull request. That
# opacity is not hypothetical — while the escapes were still raw bytes it hid the
# delimiter from `grep` and from a review agent, which then reported a confident,
# entirely fabricated hash collision built on the wrong separator.
#
# The delimiter's *value* must not change either: that would rewrite every
# imported-comment id and break the #1150 drift dedup. The escape is the same
# value. This is also the file that owns Word-comment anchor math — the one place
# a one-character error shifts every imported comment.
src/server/file-io/docx-comments.ts diff

# Binary assets -- never touch
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
